WEEK 4 – REACT FUNDAMENTALS

During Week 4, I began learning React, a JavaScript library used to build interactive user interfaces. I learned how React helps developers organize applications into reusable components and how it makes it easier to update the user interface when information changes. I studied JSX, which allows HTML-like syntax to be written inside JavaScript, and learned how to create components and combine them to build a complete application. I also learned about props, which allow information to be passed between components, and how to render lists of information using the map() method. I then studied state and the useState Hook, which allow React components to remember information and update the user interface when users interact with an application. These concepts introduced me to the basic principles of building interactive applications with React.

I began by studying what React is and why it is used in web development. React is a JavaScript library that helps developers build user interfaces by dividing an application into smaller, reusable parts. Instead of writing an entire webpage in one large block of code, developers can create separate components for different parts of the interface, such as a navigation bar, header, sidebar, button, and footer. These components can then be combined to create a complete webpage. React is useful because it makes code easier to organize, reuse, maintain, and update. It also allows the user interface to respond to changes in data without requiring the developer to manually update every affected part of the webpage.

I studied components, which are the building blocks of React applications. A component is a reusable piece of a user interface that usually returns JSX describing what should appear on the screen. Components are commonly written as JavaScript functions, and their names must begin with capital letters so that React can distinguish them from ordinary HTML elements. For example, ‘function Welcome() { return <h1>Welcome to React!</h1>; }’ creates a component named Welcome that displays a heading. To use the component, I can write ‘<Welcome />’ inside another component. This allows the same component to be reused in different places without repeating its implementation. For example, an application can display ‘<Welcome />’ several times to show the same welcome message in multiple places. Components help make applications more modular because each component can focus on a specific part of the interface.

I learned how to combine components to create a larger application. A React component can contain other components, allowing the application to be divided into logical sections. For example, I can create a Header component that displays the website heading, a Content component that displays the main information, and a Footer component that displays copyright information. An App component can then combine them by returning ‘<div><Header /><Content /><Footer /></div>’. In a real application, these components would normally be written with proper formatting across multiple lines. The important idea is that the App component acts as a parent component that brings the other components together. This structure makes the application easier to understand because each part has its own responsibility. It also makes it easier to modify one section without rewriting the entire application.

I then studied JSX, which stands for JavaScript XML. JSX is a syntax extension that allows developers to write HTML-like elements inside JavaScript code. It makes React components easier to read because the code describing the user interface is written in a structure similar to HTML. For example, ‘function App() { return <h1>Hello World!</h1>; }’ defines a component that displays a heading. Although JSX looks like HTML, it follows some different rules. For example, the HTML class attribute is written as className in JSX, and elements must be properly closed. When a component returns multiple elements, they must be placed inside one parent element or a React fragment. A fragment is written using ‘<>’ and ‘</>’ and allows multiple elements to be grouped without adding an extra HTML element to the webpage. JSX therefore provides a convenient way to describe the structure of a React user interface.

I learned how to insert JavaScript expressions inside JSX by using curly braces. This allows variables, calculations, and other JavaScript expressions to be displayed in the user interface. For example, if I declare ‘const name = "Denise";’, I can display the value using ‘<h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>’. The browser will display ‘Hello, Denise!’ because React evaluates the expression inside the curly braces. I can also perform calculations inside JSX. For example, if I declare ‘const a = 10;’ and ‘const b = 5;’, writing ‘<p>The answer is {a + b}</p>’ displays the result of the calculation. This shows that JSX can combine the structure of a webpage with dynamic information from JavaScript. I learned that curly braces are useful when a component needs to display values that may change or depend on calculations.

I studied props, which are used to pass information from a parent component to a child component. Props allow a component to receive data and use it to display different information without changing the component's implementation. For example, I can create a component using ‘function Welcome(props) { return <h1>Hello, {props.name}!</h1>; }’ and then use it as ‘<Welcome name="Denise" />’. The parent passes the value Denise through the name prop, and the Welcome component displays it. If I use ‘<Welcome name="John" />’, the same component displays John's name instead. This makes components reusable because they can receive different values depending on where they are used. Props can contain strings, numbers, arrays, objects, and functions. I also learned that props should be treated as read-only by the receiving component. A component should not directly modify its props because the parent component is responsible for supplying them.

I learned that props can be accessed using destructuring, which provides a shorter and cleaner way to work with the values passed into a component. Instead of writing ‘function Welcome(props) { return <h1>Hello, {props.name}!</h1>; }’, I can write ‘function Welcome({ name }) { return <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>; }’. Both examples display the same result, but the second example extracts the name property directly from the props object. I also learned that a component can receive several props at once. For example, a Student component can receive name, age, and course, and display all three values. Using ‘function Student({ name, age, course }) { return <div><h2>{name}</h2><p>Age: {age}</p><p>Course: {course}</p></div>; }’ allows the component to display information about different students. When using this component, string values can be passed with quotation marks, while JavaScript values such as numbers can be passed inside curly braces. Props therefore provide a flexible way of sharing information between components.

I continued learning how React displays lists of information using the JavaScript map() method. The map() method processes every item in an array and creates a new array based on the operation performed on each item. In React, it is commonly used to turn arrays of data into lists of JSX elements. For example, if I declare ‘const fruits = ["Mango", "Apple", "Banana"];’, I can display the fruits using ‘fruits.map((fruit) => <li key={fruit}>{fruit}</li>)’. Each fruit becomes a list item, and React displays the complete list. This method is useful because it allows a component to display many items without writing a separate HTML element for every item manually. If a list contains more items, the same code can render them automatically. The map() method is therefore an important technique for displaying dynamic information in React applications.

I learned about keys when rendering lists in React. A key is a special value that helps React identify individual items in a list so that it can keep track of which items have changed, been added, or been removed. For example, when displaying a list of students, each student can have a unique id, and that id can be used as the key in ‘<li key={student.id}>{student.name}</li>’. Keys should be stable and unique among the items in the same list. When a list contains objects, using an existing unique identifier is generally preferable to using the array index, especially when items can be reordered, inserted, or deleted. Without suitable keys, React may display a warning in the console and can have difficulty matching list items correctly during updates. I learned that keys help React manage lists efficiently and are an important part of rendering collections of data.

I then studied state in React. State is information that a component can remember and that may change while the application is running. Examples include a counter value, the text entered into an input field, whether a menu is open, or the number of items in a shopping cart. State is important because interactive applications need to respond to user actions and changes in information. For example, a counter may initially display zero, change to one when the user clicks an Increase button, and change to two when the button is clicked again. The changing number is state. When React state is updated, React can render the component again so that the user interface displays the latest value. This makes state different from an ordinary local variable, because changing an ordinary variable does not by itself tell React to update the interface.

I learned about the useState Hook, which allows a functional React component to manage its own state. A Hook is a special React function that allows a component to use React features. The useState Hook is imported from React and is commonly written as ‘import { useState } from "react";’. Inside a component, I can declare ‘const [count, setCount] = useState(0);’. In this example, count stores the current state value, setCount is the function used to update it, and zero is the initial value. The count variable can be displayed inside JSX using ‘<h1>Count: {count}</h1>’. When setCount is called with a new value, React updates the state and renders the component again to display the new count. This allows the user interface to respond to changes in the stored information.

I studied how to use useState to create a simple counter application. A counter can start at zero and include Increase, Decrease, and Reset buttons. The useState Hook initializes the count using ‘const [count, setCount] = useState(0);’. The Increase button can update the state using ‘onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}’, while the Decrease button can use ‘onClick={() => setCount(count - 1)}’. The Reset button can use ‘onClick={() => setCount(0)’ to return the count to zero. When the user clicks one of these buttons, the corresponding event handler runs and updates the state. React then renders the updated count. This example helped me understand how state and user interactions work together to create an interactive application. It also demonstrated that the same state value can be displayed in the interface and updated through different actions.

I learned that when a new state value depends on the previous state value, it is often best to use the functional updater form. For example, ‘setCount((previousCount) => previousCount + 1)’ calculates the next count using the previous value supplied by React. Similarly, ‘setCount((previousCount) => previousCount - 1)’ decreases the count using the previous value. This approach is useful when multiple state updates may be queued, because React can calculate each update from the correct preceding state. I also learned that state should not be modified directly. Writing ‘count = count + 1’ is not the correct way to update React state. Instead, I should use the setter function, such as setCount, so that React knows that the state has changed and can update the user interface appropriately.

I studied the relationship between event handling and state. Events are actions that occur when a user interacts with a webpage, such as clicking a button, typing into an input field, or submitting a form. React uses event handlers to specify what should happen when these actions occur. For example, ‘<button onClick={handleClick}>Click Me</button>’ tells React to call the handleClick function when the button is clicked. The function is passed to onClick without parentheses because it should run when the event occurs, rather than immediately during rendering. When an event handler updates state, React can display the result of that action. For example, clicking a button can increase a counter, change a message, or show additional content. Event handling and state are therefore important parts of building interactive React applications.

I also learned about conditional rendering, which means displaying different content depending on a condition. React components can use normal JavaScript conditions to decide what should appear on the screen. For example, an application can display a welcome message when a user is logged in and a login message when the user is not logged in. One way to achieve this is by using an if statement. Another method is the ternary operator, which follows the structure ‘condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse’. Conditional rendering can also use the logical AND operator, written as ‘&&’, when an element should appear only if a condition is true. For example, ‘{count === 10 && <p>Congratulations! You reached ten!</p>}’ displays a message when the count is exactly ten. This can be combined with useState so that the displayed content changes automatically when the state changes.

I learned how to combine components, props, state, events, and conditional rendering to build a more complete React application. For example, a counter component can use useState to store a number, display that number inside JSX, and provide buttons that update it through event handlers. It can also use conditional rendering to display a message when the number reaches a particular value. Props can be used to pass information from a parent component into the counter component, while reusable components can help organize the interface. This demonstrates how the different concepts work together rather than existing as separate ideas. Components organize the application, JSX describes the interface, props supply information, state stores changing values, event handlers respond to user actions, and conditional rendering determines what content should be displayed.

I also learned several important rules and common mistakes to avoid when working with React. Component names should begin with capital letters, JSX elements should be properly closed, and multiple returned elements should be wrapped in a parent element or a fragment. When rendering lists, each item should have an appropriate key. Props should not be modified by the receiving component, and state should be updated using its setter function rather than by directly changing the state variable. Hooks such as useState must be called at the top level of a React component or another custom Hook, not inside conditions, loops, or nested functions. Event handlers should be passed as functions rather than called immediately during rendering. Understanding these rules helps prevent common errors and makes React applications easier to maintain.

Overall, Week 4 helped me understand the fundamental concepts of React and how they are used to create interactive user interfaces. I learned what React is, how to create and combine components, how to write JSX, how to pass information using props, and how to render lists using map() and keys. I also learned how state works, how to use the useState Hook to store and update information, how to respond to user actions with event handlers, and how to display content conditionally. These concepts form an important foundation for more advanced React topics, including controlled forms, useEffect, lifting state up, component composition, custom Hooks, and building complete applications that interact with APIs.